<p>In the latest turn, UK Deputy Prime Minister David Lammy said Keir Starmer would have blocked Peter Mandelson from becoming ambassador to Washington if he had known Mandelson had failed security vetting. Lammy called it &quot;inexplicable&quot; that Oliver Robbins, the former top civil servant forced out of the Foreign Office this week, did not tell Downing Street about the result. Starmer has said he was unaware of the failed clearance. The row is becoming a political test for Starmer because Mandelson was appointed to one of Britain’s most important diplomatic posts even though Downing Street was not told about the failed vetting result. That has raised fresh questions about oversight and accountability inside government. Lammy also publicly backed Starmer, signaling support for the prime minister as the controversy intensified.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/EzS_xaQ8wtQUx1JGH4xfouSLumrP1AsLA9Kp0_ZZn0sTB-AksHkys7T7UmnMNQVFncpa1puHX94Wl1y1fChkVGL-2Ga7mGXmqNXv1_wF29At1KiAIh8ws8jiY3l_ZnsYmBXU9Ywy9BmqeEaB9fmSQ3yVVBx9pUwKscPr1ywen9_bRp1Lug5yYSzXyGf9NVYFAmMmupe8OxRsslpFJ_k5wNaPhpLyr76Apx6kearQmviXIfWQ4VHyJMrWZ1K0lQDeSvLHuNzhQexnBJLF59Wgng' alt='Image accompanying coverage of the Mandelson vetting dispute' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Blame shift: The argument is no longer just about Mandelson’s failed vetting. Lammy’s remarks put Oliver Robbins and the government’s internal information chain at the center of the dispute.</li><li>First intervention: The Guardian described these as Lammy’s first public comments on the affair, while the Straits Times framed them as a clear show of support for Starmer as pressure mounted.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>David Lammy: Lammy centered his criticism on the communication failure inside government, saying it was &quot;inexplicable&quot; that Oliver Robbins did not tell Downing Street about the vetting result before the appointment. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2026/apr/18/starmer-would-have-blocked-mandelson-role-over-vetting-failure-says-lammy'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Keir Starmer: Starmer’s position, as reflected in the coverage, is that the appointment went ahead without him being told Mandelson had failed security clearance. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/video/inside-story/2026/4/18/will-keir-starmer-resign'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li><li>Al Jazeera: The broadcaster framed the row as a broader test of political accountability for the prime minister, not just a dispute over one diplomatic posting. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/video/inside-story/2026/4/18/will-keir-starmer-resign'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2026/apr/18/starmer-would-have-blocked-mandelson-role-over-vetting-failure-says-lammy'>Starmer would have blocked Mandelson role over vetting failure, says Lammy</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/video/inside-story/2026/4/18/will-keir-starmer-resign'>Will Keir Starmer resign?</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/europe/uks-lammy-signals-support-for-pm-starmer-over-latest-mandelson-row'>UK&#x27;s Lammy signals support for PM Starmer over latest Mandelson row</a> - straitstimes.com</li></ul>